{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T001529Z-68684a21", "ts": "2026-11-26T00:19:02Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.0", "branch": "fix/boundary-coverage-transfer", "build": "e9c06c071287"}, "body": {"base_commit": "4dffd694152233daab3d2e960481e3e5327da102", "base_tree": "30cfafb36a5cbf42b2e787d4d009325325ca9eda", "head_tree": "367c5429c6605e3d906a177eafc7d32f72970d56", "head_commit": "899a368be129452cadf333abf35c32728e81daa3", "dispatch_commit": "899a368be129452cadf333abf35c32728e81daa3", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "blocking", "title": "pr-transferred widens the owner-ruled 'covered at its PR' definition that #1232 will gate on, with no recorded decision", "recommendation": "The 2026-11-24 ruling in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md accepted wave 1's 'covered' definition as written, and said wave 2 (#1232) gates on it: 'A merge is covered at its PR when a boundary-stage review read the branch from where it left the integration branch, and only verify-resolutions passes (and free edges) composed what followed. The composition over all facts must also read covered.' The accepted design text (documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md section 3) also names 'branches not synced before review' as a known break. This change adds PR_TRANSFERRED to boundary_coverage.COVERED. That class covers exactly the merges where no review read the branch from its merge-time merge-base, and where composition over all facts reads `uncovered`; the base-advance transfer is what carries them. Making the PR gate's transfer count as boundary coverage may be the right call. But it is edge-work on a ruled definition that a future gate will enforce. The change fragment's 'the op asks the owner-ruled boundary question of the span the coverage came from' is a builder inference, and norms.md says a builder-authored rationale alone is not a recorded decision. No #1250 ruling exists in owner-rulings.md, and the #1247-cluster ruling in base commit 4dffd694 does not mention it. Remedy: record the owner's ruling at the edge of the 2026-11-24 definition, in owner-rulings.md or as a vetoable [DECISION] the owner confirms. It should say that a merge the PR gate passed by base-advance transfer, whose prior span passes the boundary-plus-verify split, counts as covered for the release check and for #1232's gate. The code needs no change. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A classification added to boundary_coverage.COVERED that the 2026-11-24 ruled definition does not already cover.", "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The release op asks about the branch tip with a suite lookup built for the current worktree, so a branch the PR gate passed by transfer still reads uncovered when its last run preceded a records commit", "recommendation": "base_advance_transfer's docstring and the change record both promise that a branch the PR gate passed by transfer cannot read as unreviewed at the release. That holds only when a test-run fact's tree is byte-identical to the branch tip. At the PR gate, suite_vouches_for_tree(head_tree) runs in the branch's own checkout. There it accepts this worktree's .test-evidence.json, and the store's newest runs for the branch and HEAD, whenever only paths that cannot change a test outcome differ (_tree_met's tolerance; a non-protected .md record commit is one such path). At the release, _store_run_vouching looks up only three candidates: the exact target tree, the release checkout's HEAD, and the release checkout's branch (develop). A branch whose last suite run came before a records-only or doc commit therefore never surfaces as a candidate. TRANSFER_SUITE_STALE follows, and the scope reads `uncovered`: 'no saved suite run met the branch tip'. That is #1250's own symptom: a NOTE calling reviewed work unreviewed, with a remedy (run the suite on an already-merged tip) that the PR gate never needed. The exposure is real. Of the last 40 first-parent merges on develop, 20 have no test-run fact whose tree equals their ^2 tree (queried against this clone's store). Second, the release checkout's own local record is also a candidate. If it is newest, carries no tree and is refused (a suite in flight, which the real-store test's docstring acknowledges 'denies every tree by design'), it denies every transferred scope at once. Every fixture records the run on the exact tip tree (_suite_ran after the records commit), so this path is untested. This is the hook-surface rule 'A guard's TOLERANCES belong to the path it was written for': suite_vouches_for_tree was written to answer about this worktree's current tree. Remedy (class): give the release op a tip-scoped run lookup, for example test-run facts whose head commit is in merge_base..tip, compared with _tree_met. Exclude the release checkout's local record from a question about a historical tree. Add a fixture where the run precedes a records-only commit on the branch.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "Any release-op suite question about a merged branch's tip that resolves its candidate runs from the release checkout's HEAD, branch or local record rather than from the merged branch's own runs.", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "pr-transferred depends on a suite run recorded in the same clone, so a release cut in another clone still reads a transferred PR as uncovered", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the op makes the PR gate's decision again, later and possibly somewhere else, and that decision rests on condition 3. Condition 3 reads `test-run` facts and `.test-evidence.json`, and evidence.py keeps both out of TRANSPORTED_KINDS ('a test-run is a suite result about the machine and environment that ran it'). Reviews and resolutions travel on refs/notes/prawduct, so `pr-covered` gives the same answer in every clone; `pr-transferred` does not. Picture a product whose PRs pass the gate in a contributor's clone and whose release is cut in the maintainer's. There `_transferred` returns TRANSFER_SUITE_STALE and the merge reads `uncovered`, with the reason 'no saved suite run met the branch tip'. That is the false 'unreviewed' NOTE #1250 set out to remove. release-process.md tells the operator to treat it as work no boundary review read, and its remedy, a suite run against a branch tip that has already been merged, is not something the operator can sensibly do at a release. The api-contract describes this as 'the one decision both read', but where the inputs live decides the answer. Pick one: (a) carry the grant across clones (the PR gate's grant, or the test-run that satisfied it) as transported evidence and have the op read it; or (b) at minimum, have the suite-stale reason say the run may be recorded in another clone, as the plain-uncovered reason already does for reviews ('evidence sync brings the reviews here'), and state the clone-local limit where api-contract.md describes `pr-transferred`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The release op's reading of the transfer's suite condition, the one input of the transferred decision that does not travel between clones.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py:_Classifier._transferred"}], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The boundary-patterns contract for the transfer still lists only the two gates as its consumers; the decision now has one owner, base_advance_transfer, and a third reader", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the change moved the transfer decision into `gates.base_advance_transfer` and added a reader in another module. `.prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md` 'Diagnosis Status Verdicts (the two review gates)' still names the PR gate and the Stop gate (plus transfer_remedy) as the consumers. It does not mention base_advance_transfer's own envelope (`decision`/`kind`/`transfer`/`tests_reason`, TRANSFER_GRANTED/SUITE_STALE/DENIED) or `boundary_coverage`, which branches on `gates.TRANSFER_*` and reads the match-only fields `prior_base`/`prior_head`. A maintainer who changes that envelope or the diagnosis's match fields uses this registry to find consumers and will miss the release op. A slip there makes check-boundary-coverage `unevaluable` (classify_pending catches the exception) rather than failing loudly anywhere. Update that section so the decision site is `gates.base_advance_transfer`, list its three callers, and record its decision envelope as the contract the callers branch on. Optional: architecture.md:226 also cites `coverage.diagnose_base_advance_transfer` as the transfer. That citation still points at where the conditions live, but no longer at the decision.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The contract-registry entry that describes the transfer decision's consumers.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md: Diagnosis Status Verdicts"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "pr-transferred widens the owner-ruled release meaning of 'covered', and nothing records an owner ruling for it", "recommendation": "The owner ruling of 2026-11-24 in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md defines a merge as covered at its PR when 'a boundary-stage review read the branch from where it left the integration branch, and only verify-resolutions passes (and free edges) composed what followed. The composition over all facts must also read covered.' It also says wave 2 (#1232) gates on that definition. pr-transferred is a merge where neither clause holds: the boundary review read the branch from its old merge-base, and the composition over all facts reads uncovered. Yet COVERED now includes it. The reasoning (the PR gate passes this shape by transfer, so the release should agree) is written down only in the change fragment and the module docstring. #1250 offered two options: honour the transfer, or report a distinct class. The builder took both and counted the class as covered, and nothing records the owner choosing that. The consequence: once #1232 turns this advisory op into a gate, transferred merges will pass the release on a definition the owner never ruled, and the go/no-go counts for the discodon run (ruling of 2026-11-25, item 1) will include them as covered. Fix: put the extension to the owner. Record the answer beneath the 2026-11-24 ruling, leaving that ruling's text unchanged, or in #1232's plan Goals, where wave 2 reads its definition. Until then, mark the 'counts as covered' choice as inferred. Scope: instance. One classification widens one ruled definition.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A release-coverage classification counted in boundary_coverage.COVERED that the 2026-11-24 owner ruling's definition of covered does not admit.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py:COVERED (PR_TRANSFERRED)"}], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 4, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "An `unavailable` transfer diagnosis at the release reads as plain 'no review evidence composes'", "recommendation": "_transferred returns None for TRANSFER_DENIED of any kind, so a transfer check that could not run (pruned trees, kind `unavailable`) prints the same reason as one that did not match. The Stop and PR gates name the could-not-run case (transfer_remedy). It is advisory output, and it fails toward `uncovered`, so this is noted only.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: nothing to reconcile", "recommendation": "No plan claims this branch (scope unbound), so there is no governed_by: disposition to test against the diff. The PR- and Stop-gate refactor onto gates.base_advance_transfer was checked against the pre-change code and preserves behavior: same suite_tree per gate, same transfer_stale and transfer_note branches, same grant recording.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "suite_vouches_for_tree's docstring still calls the transfer coverage.diagnose_base_advance_transfer and credits it with a third condition", "recommendation": "gates.py:912-913 says 'the base-advance transfer (coverage.diagnose_base_advance_transfer), whose third condition...'. That function's own docstring says condition 3 belongs to the caller, and the transfer decision is now base_advance_transfer. The wording is inert and nothing reads it; if the docstring is touched for another reason, make the reference relational (':func:`base_advance_transfer`'). No edit is wanted for its own sake.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "_merge_base_verdict's docstring still argues from suite_vouches_for_tree's default, but the call now passes suite_tree=None explicitly", "recommendation": "'condition 3 lands MORE squarely here ... because suite_vouches_for_tree defaults to the working tree' is still true of the callee, but the call site no longer relies on that default, which base_advance_transfer deliberately refuses to give. This is inert prose and needs no edit.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "pr-transferred's prose says the PR gate passed the merge, but the op re-derives that answer and never observes the gate", "recommendation": "The boundary_coverage docstring, the api-contract and the change note describe pr-transferred as a merge 'the PR gate passed by its base-advance transfer'. The op actually computes whether the transfer would hold now. It does not read the recorded grant (record_transfer_grant), so a hand-merged branch that satisfies the conditions also reads pr-transferred. `pr-covered` has the same naming convention, so this is consistent and I am not asking for a change.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The suite-stale reason implies a suite run would cover the merge even when the prior span is inner-only", "recommendation": "_transferred returns the TRANSFER_SUITE_STALE reason before it applies the prior span's boundary split. A chunk-only branch with no suite run therefore reads 'transfer matched ... no saved suite run met the branch tip', when a suite run would only lift it to inner-only. The message is advisory and its facts are correct, so this is borderline. The decision could be split first, so the reason names the remedy that would actually cover it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The release op folds an 'unavailable' transfer into the plain 'no review evidence composes' reason, which drops the distinction both gates keep", "recommendation": "_Classifier._transferred returns None for every TRANSFER_DENIED, so kind == 'unavailable' (the transfer check could not run) reads the same as 'no review exists'. It also prints the 'evidence sync brings the reviews here' remedy. Both gates render that kind through transfer_remedy for the reason coverage.diagnose_base_advance_transfer states: a degraded check that says nothing looks the same as one that found nothing. At release, a reachable unavailable needs a git failure that the earlier classification steps did not already hit, so this is rare, and the op is advisory. A separate uncovered reason for 'unavailable' would close it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "base_advance_transfer's docstring states an absolute guarantee that the release op cannot keep", "recommendation": "The docstring says 'a branch the PR gate passed by transfer cannot read as unreviewed at the release'. The release op re-decides the transfer from the evidence present at release time, not from the PR gate's recorded grant. A newer red test-run on the same tree, a store not synced into the clone doing the cut, or a base that moved again between the gate and the merge can each deny it. Nothing reads the sentence. Make it relational ('asks the same decision') rather than a guarantee. No other edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: covered .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md and .prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md, and neither clears a bar", "recommendation": "Both oracle files were read against the diff. The fragment's shipped consumer note checks out against the code: pr-transferred counts as covered, `counts` has a new key, a suite-stale near miss reads uncovered with the suite named, and transferred inner-only coverage stays inner-only. The api-contract phrase 'that the PR gate passed by its base-advance transfer' describes a recomputation as if it read the gate's recorded grant. That is imprecise but misleads no operator into a wrong action. No edit is wanted.", "oid": "O-9"}], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/boundary-coverage-transfer`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "4dffd694152233daab3d2e960481e3e5327da102", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0, "test-scope-conftest": 0, "test-scope-autouse": 0, "test-scope-init": 0, "test-clock-fake": 0, "test-clock-import": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-26T00:15:34Z"}}
